WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.729 --> 00:00:22.231
Hi there, I'm Jasha Klebe,

00:00:22.314 --> 00:00:25.025
and I'm the composer for
the Netflix series Animal.

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:26.944
Today,
I'd like to share with you

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:28.320
one of my personal
favourite moments

00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:29.696
in the octopus episode

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.657
and how I crafted the music.

00:00:34.284 --> 00:00:36.578
I set the scene
by characterising the ocean

00:00:36.662 --> 00:00:38.247
with a wash
of ambient guitars

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.123
and swirling synths
and organs.

00:00:43.794 --> 00:00:45.295
Creating
this rippling motion,

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:46.672
that provides
a sense of mystery

00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:48.382
as we descent deeper.

00:00:49.925 --> 00:00:52.135
Just as the octopus eggs
come into frame,

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:53.303
I signify them

00:00:53.387 --> 00:00:55.597
with these flickering
sonar-type sounds.

00:00:56.431 --> 00:00:58.475
I wanted to convey
these little bursts of life

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:00.143
that are just waiting
to hatch.

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.524
It's here I introduce
a long melodic whistle

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:07.067
that becomes
a signature sound

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.694
for a giant pacific octopus.

00:01:13.824 --> 00:01:15.534
There's a familiarity
and intimacy

00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:16.618
to the sound of a whistle

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:19.162
and I wanted to use
that to help us,

00:01:19.246 --> 00:01:20.831
as the audience,
to connect with an animal

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:23.500
that's often portrayed
as strange and alien-like.

00:01:27.171 --> 00:01:28.088
At this moment,

00:01:28.172 --> 00:01:30.340
the mother octopus
is shown blowing on the eggs

00:01:30.424 --> 00:01:31.842
to keep them alive

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.678
and I introduce a tremolo
of textural-type-strings

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.680
to mimic this airy quality.

00:01:41.059 --> 00:01:42.352
Again, we hear the whistle,

00:01:42.436 --> 00:01:44.688
almost as a call from the
mother to her babies saying

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:45.981
"It's time to hatch."

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.910
Using a frenzy of pumping
heart-like synths,

00:01:57.993 --> 00:01:59.703
I match stylized editing,

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:01.371
as we are provided
with a glimpse

00:02:01.455 --> 00:02:03.582
into what could be
this baby octopus's future

00:02:03.665 --> 00:02:04.583
if they survive.

00:02:07.002 --> 00:02:08.462
We now get our
baby octopus's theme

00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:09.379
for the first time.

00:02:15.344 --> 00:02:17.262
It begins with a simple
waltz-like motive

00:02:17.346 --> 00:02:18.680
on an electric piano.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:21.475
I found inspiration
in the gentle,

00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:23.143
pulsing motion of
the baby octopuses

00:02:23.227 --> 00:02:25.646
as if they drift
through the ocean currents.

00:02:25.729 --> 00:02:27.606
Almost like
choreographing a dance.

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:40.077
I build on this with the
return of our lone whistle.

00:02:41.203 --> 00:02:42.579
Across this episode,

00:02:42.663 --> 00:02:44.039
we follow
this giant pacific octopus

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:45.457
from hatching to adulthood

00:02:46.041 --> 00:02:48.168
and I wanted to give her
this distinct sound state

00:02:48.252 --> 00:02:49.127
that we could recognise

00:02:49.211 --> 00:02:51.171
each time
we return to her story.

00:02:52.339 --> 00:02:54.049
We learn that once hatched,

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:57.219
a giant pacific octopus
never sees her mother again.

00:02:57.302 --> 00:03:00.013
So I looked at this moment
almost as a goodbye song,

00:03:00.556 --> 00:03:02.015
from a mother to her child,

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:05.018
as we witness one cycle
of life nears its end

00:03:05.102 --> 00:03:06.144
and another begins.

00:03:08.272 --> 00:03:11.483
Quiet swells of choir
and strings gently expand

00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:13.110
and become large in scope

00:03:14.903 --> 00:03:16.071
as our baby octopus

00:03:16.154 --> 00:03:18.782
makes her way into the vast
ocean for the first time.

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:30.335
The scene ends back
in our simple waltz-motive,

00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:33.255
reminding us that this
is but one octopus's journey

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:34.423
in this large ocean.

